    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/berea/jobs/5463567/care-management-bereacorps-associate": {
      "title": "Care Management BereaCorps Associate",
      "description": "Job Opportunities | Berea College Careers For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. \n        Here are the instructions how to enable JavaScript in your web browser Care Management BereaCorps Associate | Berea College Careers page has loaded. Care Management BereaCorps Associate | Berea College Careers page has loaded. Click here or press any key to continue. Job Opportunities Menu Job Opportunities Sign In Don't have an account? Create one . Already have an account? Sign In . User profile dropdown menu for Username Applications Inbox Profile Account Sign Out After clicking on this link you will be logged out and the page will be reloaded. Applications Inbox Profile Go to Background Investigation Account Sign Out After clicking on this link you will be logged out and the page will be reloaded. Welcome! Thanks for creating an account. Berea College Careers powered by NEOGOV ® Need this page in another language? You can translate this page using your browser's built-in translation feature. Tip: In Chrome or Edge, right-click anywhere on the page and select \"Translate to [Language].\" Care Management BereaCorps Associate Print Apply ﻿ Care Management BereaCorps Associate Location Berea Job Type Full-Time Division Student Life Opening Date 08/27/2026 FLSA Non-Exempt Labor Supervisor? Yes Description Benefits Please note that references provided at the time of application for staff positions will not be contacted until the applicant has been offered the position. Description: Reporting to the Care Manager, the Care Management Support Associate will serve as a member of the Care Management team within Counseling Services, providing non-clinical support, resource navigation, and referral assistance to Berea College students. The position will assist students in identifying and addressing basic needs and other barriers that may affect their academic success, sense of belonging, and progress toward graduation. The Associate will receive comprehensive training, shadowing, and progressively independent, supervised experience in Care Management practices. Working within established Care Management processes and under the guidance of the Care Manager, the Associate will meet with students to identify needs, connect students with campus and community resources, facilitate referrals, and provide follow-up support. The Associate will work collaboratively with the Care Manager and other campus partners to support students and will refer students to the Care Manager or other appropriate professional resources when needs fall outside the scope of non-clinical Care Management. The position will also assist with Care Management program development, student outreach, and the work of the Health Education Advocacy Resource Team (HEART). Through structured training, supervision, and experiential learning, the position is designed to provide a recent Berea College graduate with opportunities to develop professional skills in student support, resource navigation, advocacy, program development, and higher education. Your Key Responsibilities: To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. The requirements listed below are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. Regular and predictable attendance is essential. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. Direct Student Support – 65% (26 hrs./week) Participate in student intake appointments and conduct basic needs assessments using established Care Management processes, tools, and protocols under the guidance and supervision of the Care Manager. Facilitate collaborative appointments with students to identify needs, goals, and barriers to success, and assist students in developing individualized service plans. Connect students to appropriate campus and community resources to address identified needs, including housing, food access, social benefits, financial resources, clothing or durable goods, and other basic needs. Provide resource navigation, referral coordination, follow-up communication, and other non-clinical support to students within the scope of Care Management services. Serve as an initial point of contact for students seeking support and promptly connect students with the Care Manager or other appropriate professional resources when the concerns exceed the scope of non-clinical Care Management. Advocate for students and assist them with navigating institutional and external service systems within the scope of the position. Maintain regular communication with assigned students and provide follow-up support and monitor progress toward identified goals and needs. Develop increasing independence in managing assigned clients through structured training, supervision, consultation, and ongoing professional development. Health Education Advocacy Resource Team – 25% (10 hrs./week) Assist with programmatic development and day-to-day operations in collaboration with the Health Education Advocacy Resource Team (HEART) Labor Students and Supervisor. Coordinate completion of the 2024 Work College Consortium grant awarded to HEART, with guidance from the HEART Supervisor and Care Manager, including preparation for the final public presentation. Administrative – 10% (4 hrs./week) Maintain detailed and accurate case files, including but not limited to, tracking client contacts, referrals, and care management progress in accordance with FERPA, HIPAA, and departmental standards. Participate in Care Management request for services meetings, case consultations, and regular supervision with Care Manager. Maintain an online presence for Care Management via the student engagement platform, CampusGroups, and facilitate student outreach, engagement, and referrals through the platform, as appropriate. Participate in professional development, training, and ongoing learning opportunities related to student support, resource navigation, crisis response protocols, and Care Management practices. In support of Berea College’s Great Commitments, this position requires student supervision.  The fourth Great Commitment is to promote learning and serving in the community through the student labor program, honoring the dignity and utility of all work, mental and manual, and taking pride in work well done. Student Supervision includes scheduling, assigning, and approving work performed. Developing students' common workplace acumen and giving them tangible work experience is an expected outcome. To learn more about the student labor program and requirements for this position, see: Labor Program Goals Other roles/duties will be assigned as necessary to assist the College in the attainment of the goals set forth and the enhancement of a positive, respectful learning environment for all staff, faculty and students. What You'll Bring: Education required to ensure success in this position: Bachelor’s Degree from Berea College with a graduation date in Calendar Years 2025 or 2026. Major in Child and Family Studies, Health Studies, Nursing, Psychology, Sociology, Women’s, Gender, and Sexuality Studies, or a related field preferred. Experience required to ensure success in this position: Experience through student leadership, internships, employment, volunteer service, or other activities involving student- or client-facing interactions, Experience working collaboratively with individuals from diverse backgrounds and perspectives. Demonstrated ability to communicate effectively, build relationships, and provide excellent customer service. Experience or demonstrated interest in supporting individuals in navigating resources, services, or systems. Demonstrated critical thinking, problem-solving, organization, and conflict resolution skills. Ability to work both independently and collaboratively, take initiative, and seek guidance when appropriate. Excellent written and oral communication skills. Experience managing private or confidential student or client records. Adaptability, flexibility, and willingness to learn new skills and processes. Special skills, knowledge and abilities: Demonstrated interest in supporting students' overall well-being, success, and ability to navigate challenges and access resources. Superior interpersonal skills. Highly organized, self-motivated, and productive. Proficient in Microsoft Office Suite programs and online learning and productivity platforms. Ability and willingness to learn, develop, and grow as a leader and professional. Interest in graduate study or a career in Social Work, Counseling, Healthcare, Human Services, Program Management, or Higher Education. License, certification, or registration necessary: Completion of the Kentucky State required Core Competencies: Targeted Case Management training within first 6 months of employment. Certified Peer Educator and QPR Training for Suicide Prevention preferred. Physical requirements: Ability to use a computer for extended periods. Ability to navigate campus, buildings, and grounds. Ability to drive a motor vehicle and a valid driver’s license. Ability to stand for long periods of time. Environmental conditions: Professional office environment with interruptions and noise due to frequent visitors. Occasional travel is required for off-campus events, meetings, and conferences. A flexible work schedule is permitted with approval from direct supervisor or Divisional leadership. Ability to traverse campus to meet with students, faculty, staff, alumni, community members, and guests. Ability to operate the following vehicles or equipment: Computer Standard office equipment Motor vehicle and/or a golf cart Founded in 1855, Berea College is nationally recognized as the first coeducational and interracial college in the South. Berea has a longstanding commitment to interracial education and is one of the most racially diverse private liberal arts colleges in the United States. With an emphasis on service to Appalachia and beyond, Berea enrolls more than 1,500 students from 44 states and U.S. territories and more than 60 countries. Berea College admits students who are unable to afford tuition and provides every enrolled student with a fully funded degree that covers 100% of tuition, housing, food, supplies, and fees without loans, for all four years. Berea’s students excel in the College’s supportive yet demanding academic environment, and most are the first in their families to attend college. As one of only 10 federally recognized Work Colleges, all Berea students work 10-12 hours weekly in various positions across campus, building professional skills in a real-world learning environment. Washington Monthly ranked Berea College the #1 Best College for your Tuition and Tax Dollars, 2025. Berea also ranks #1 in educational access by The New York Times , 2023 and #1 for lowest educational debt by U.S. News and World Report , 2025 and 2026. Berea College is also the only institution in Kentucky to receive the 5-Star Rating from Money Magazine, 2026. Located where the Bluegrass Region meets the Cumberland Mountains, the town of Berea (pop. 17,000) lies 40 miles south of Lexington and is approximately two hours from Cincinnati, Louisville, and Knoxville. More information about Berea College is available at www.berea.edu . Berea College, in light of its mission in the tradition of impartial love and social equality, welcomes all people of the earth to learn and work here. Our Benefits: Berea College offers a comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees and their families including health care, dental, vision, retirement, health savings accounts, flexible spending accounts, life insurance, short-term disability, long-term disability and an Employee Assistance Program. The benefit package also includes access to the Child Development Laboratory for children of College Staff, ages 6 weeks to 6 years, Seabury Center Membership, Staff Development Funds and Tuition Reimbursement. Eligible staff employees also receive paid holidays, vacation, and sick leave. Part-time employees who work an average of twenty-four hours per week over the course of a year, receive benefits on a pro-rated basis. To learn more details, visit our benefits page at https://www.berea.edu/human-resources/employee-benefits . Our Unique Culture: Berea College strives to be a place where people with various Christian interpretations, different religious traditions, and no religious traditions work together in support of Berea's Great Commitments. Over the past century, various leaders of the College have applied the College’s inclusive scriptural foundation and spirit to their expanding world and welcomed those whose beliefs were consistent with the Christian gospel of impartial love. Therefore, Berea College today affirms its inclusive Christian tradition even as it respects the traditions of Jews, Muslims, Buddhists, and Hindus, as well as other faiths, and those holding no religious beliefs. We strive not to ignore our differences, but rather seek to understand each other honestly and respectfully, and together create a climate where anyone can openly discuss what they believe without fear of sanction. To that end, all persons who are willing to share in the spirit and the work of the Great Commitments as shaped by its preamble are welcome to study, to teach, and to work at Berea Labor Supervision: In support of Berea College’s great commitments, staff and faculty serve as labor supervisors. Our fourth great commitment, The Dignity of Labor, promotes learning and serving in community through the student Labor Program, honoring the dignity and utility of all work, mental and manual, and taking pride in work well done. Student Labor Supervision includes scheduling, assigning, and approving student work, developing students' workplace acumen, and providing tangible work experience. To learn more about the labor program office please see the following link Labor Program Office. Employer Berea College Address 101 Chestnut St. Berea, Kentucky, 40404 Website http://www.berea.edu/ Apply Please verify your email address Verify Email Job Opportunities Account Privacy Policy Terms Of Use Accessibility Statement Privacy Settings Copyright © 2026 Do not sell my personal information × For California residents, and to the extent NEOGOV sells your personal information as the term “sell” is defined under the California Consumer Privacy Act, you have the right to opt-out of the sale of your personal information by us to third parties at any time. This setting only applies to registered account holders that have opted-in for an Access Membership. If you wish to opt-out click follow these steps: Log into your Job Seeker Profile Click your name in the upper right-hand corner Select Account Settings Select the Preferences section Click the Edit button next to Access Membership Switch Toggle to off Click Save If you do not have a registered account this setting does not apply, however, you can review your right on our privacy policy . If you require further assistance, please email privacy@neogov.com × Accessibility Statement At Governmentjobs.com, Inc. d/b/a NEOGOV, we recognize the importance of making our products\n                    and services accessible to all individuals, including those with disabilities, and are continually\n                    working to improve the experience for all users. Commitment to Accessibility We are committed to following best practices, including the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines\n                    (WCAG) 2.1, Level AA, to the extent feasible, however, we do not warrant compliance with these\n                    standards or any other accessibility standards. We are continually enhancing our platforms to\n                    ensure greater accessibility, and we actively seek out opportunities to improve accessibility features. On