    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/berea/jobs/5444429/work-college-initiatives-coordinator": {
      "title": "Work College Initiatives Coordinator",
      "description": "Job Opportunities | Berea College Careers For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. \n        Here are the instructions how to enable JavaScript in your web browser Work College Initiatives Coordinator | Berea College Careers page has loaded. Work College Initiatives Coordinator | Berea College Careers page has loaded. Click here or press any key to continue. Job Opportunities Menu Job Opportunities Sign In Don't have an account? Create one . Already have an account? Sign In . User profile dropdown menu for Username Applications Inbox Profile Account Sign Out After clicking on this link you will be logged out and the page will be reloaded. Applications Inbox Profile Go to Background Investigation Account Sign Out After clicking on this link you will be logged out and the page will be reloaded. Welcome! Thanks for creating an account. Berea College Careers powered by NEOGOV ® Need this page in another language? You can translate this page using your browser's built-in translation feature. Tip: In Chrome or Edge, right-click anywhere on the page and select \"Translate to [Language].\" Work College Initiatives Coordinator Print Apply ﻿ Work College Initiatives Coordinator Location Berea Job Type Full-Time Division Labor Program Opening Date 08/11/2026 FLSA Exempt Salary Grade TBD Labor Supervisor? Yes Description Benefits Please note that references provided at the time of application for staff positions will not be contacted until the applicant has been offered the position. Description: Under the general direction of the Dean, the Work College Initiatives Coordinator provides project management and coordination support across Work College Program priorities and oversees the coordination of financial capabilities education through the MoneyWi$e initiative. The role is divided approximately evenly between these two areas of responsibility: project management and coordination (including support for assigned projects) make up approximately 50% of the role, and the MoneyWi$e initiative makes up the other approximately 50%. These percentages are intended as planning guidance rather than a fixed allocation, and the balance of time will shift from term to term based on programmatic needs and institutional priorities. In the project management capacity, the Coordinator serves as project manager for cross-functional Work College Program initiatives and provides support on assigned projects, helping align efforts, track shared goals, and strengthen the connection between students' academic and work-learning experiences. In the MoneyWi$e capacity, the Coordinator is responsible for creating and implementing the financial wellness program and services for students on campus and consults regularly with relevant faculty and industry professionals to ensure the curriculum and programming remain current and relevant. More broadly, the Coordinator aligns their duties with data analysis and other Work College Program duties to evaluate the overall effectiveness of the program in achieving its goals. Following long-standing tradition and practice at Berea College, this position includes responsibility for supervising, mentoring, and developing students in the College's integrated Work College Program. This supports student learning, career readiness, and the program's learning goals and outcomes. The Work College Program provides a developmental framework that guides student learning while creating a structured mentorship opportunity, with role-specific training that equips faculty and staff mentors to support student development. Faculty and staff mentors play an essential part in helping students connect their work-learning-service experiences with broader academic, professional, and personal development. For faculty, this aligns with Berea's tenure and promotion criteria. Your Key Responsibilities: The Academic Affairs Collaboration duties below, together with project management and coordination duties within the Work College Program section below, make up approximately 50% of the role. The MoneyWi$e related duties within the Work College Program section make up the other approximately 50%. These figures are approximate and will shift over time based on programmatic and institutional priorities. Academic Affairs Collaboration Duties (in support of the Dean of the Work College Program) Support the Dean for the Work College Program on assigned projects within the Work College Program’s collaboration with the Division of Academic Affairs, which the Dean primarily leads, including participating in planning meetings as needed to help coordinate timelines. Partner with Academic Affairs staff on assigned collaborative projects to help align student support efforts and identify shared priorities across the two divisions. Assist in developing and implementing joint programming, communications, and referral pathways between the Work College Program and Academic Affairs. Track progress and outcomes on assigned Work College Program/Academic Affairs projects. Work College Program Duties MoneyWi$e Financial Capabilities (approximately 50% of role): Serve as the primary point of contact for students participating in the MoneyWi$e program, tracking their progress, participation, and program completion. Maintain and continue implementing the peer-to-peer model that offers one-on-one financial literacy counseling to students by students, including recruiting, training, and overseeing student peer counselors. Lead the development and implementation of the financial wellness program and services for students on campus, including workshops, seminars, one-on-one counseling sessions, and open labor meeting workshops on financial wellness. Maintain up-to-date knowledge of financial capabilities best practices and trends, consulting regularly with relevant faculty and industry professionals to ensure the MoneyWi$e curriculum and programming remain current and relevant. Collect and analyze data and metrics — including student satisfaction surveys developed with the Office of Institutional Research — to evaluate the effectiveness of financial literacy programs and services and make recommendations for improvements. Contribute to the creation of educational resources and materials, such as online tutorials, printed guides, and multimedia content, to help educate students about financial wellness topics. Following long-standing tradition and practice at Berea College, this position includes responsibility for supervising, mentoring, and developing students in the College's integrated Work College Program. This supports student learning, career readiness, and the program's learning goals and outcomes. The Work College Program provides a developmental framework that guides student learning while creating a structured mentorship opportunity, with role-specific training that equips faculty and staff mentors to support student development. Faculty and staff mentors play an essential part in helping students connect their work-learning-service experiences with broader academic, professional, and personal development. For faculty, this aligns with Berea's tenure and promotion criteria. Project Management & Program Coordination (approximately 50% of role, combined with Academic Affairs Collaboration Duties above): Serve as project manager for cross-functional Work College Program initiatives — developing project plans, timelines, and milestones; coordinating logistics, scheduling, and resource planning; and reporting progress to the Dean for the Work College Program. Maintain project documentation and a shared tracking system, including status reports and action items, to ensure visibility and accountability across the Work College Program initiatives, and proactively identify and resolve project risks and bottlenecks with relevant stakeholders. Serve as a liaison between Academics, Financial Aid, and the Work College Program, educating students on how financial aid and the Work College Program connect to ensure comprehensive support. Collect and analyze data and metrics to evaluate the effectiveness of the Work College Program, including learning outcomes and progress toward program goals. What You'll Bring: Education required to ensure success in this position: Bachelor's degree in business administration, higher education, finance, or a related field. Experience required to ensure success in this position: Willing to work as needed beyond hours to host, coordinate, or assist in program events in the evenings and some weekends during the academic term. Special skills, knowledge and abilities: Excellent interpersonal and communication skills (written and oral) Strong organizational and problem-solving skills Attention to detail Ability to work independently on multiple, complex administrative tasks and priorities Ability to respond quickly and appropriately to faculty, staff and student concerns, addressing issues independently whenever possible and referring them to others when needed Ability to develop and update resource materials for the workplace Proficiency with technology (e.g. Microsoft Office Programs, Box, Banner, Qualtrics) Professional manner and ability to handle confidential information Collaborative team-based working style and participatory leadership style are essential Demonstrated project management skills, including the ability to develop timelines, track deliverables, and coordinate multiple concurrent initiatives to completion Ability to build and sustain effective cross-departmental partnerships, particularly with academic units Experience with data analysis and evaluation Ability to maintain confidentiality and handle sensitive information License, certification, or registration necessary: Valid driver’s license Required background checks Physical requirements: Ability to work independently and as part of a team. Must be able to use a computer keyboard and read a computer screen. Requires clear oral communication by telephone and in face-to-face conversation. Ability to maintain a high level of energy is a plus. Some light lifting required Ability to sit for long periods of time with computer usage Ability to operate office equipment Ability to travel by car and/or plane Environmental conditions: Indoor fast-paced office environment and out of office visitation and support of ~100 labor departments with diverse work environments. Ability to operate the following vehicles or equipment: Vehicle College Vehicle Standard office equipment, including computer, fax machine, copier, printer. Founded in 1855, Berea College is nationally recognized as the first coeducational and interracial college in the South. Berea has a longstanding commitment to interracial education and is one of the most racially diverse private liberal arts colleges in the United States. With an emphasis on service to Appalachia and beyond, Berea enrolls more than 1,500 students from 44 states and U.S. territories and more than 60 countries. Berea College admits students who are unable to afford tuition and provides every enrolled student with a fully funded degree that covers 100% of tuition, housing, food, supplies, and fees without loans, for all four years. Berea’s students excel in the College’s supportive yet demanding academic environment, and most are the first in their families to attend college. As one of only 10 federally recognized Work Colleges, all Berea students work 10-12 hours weekly in various positions across campus, building professional skills in a real-world learning environment. Washington Monthly ranked Berea College the #1 Best College for your Tuition and Tax Dollars, 2025. Berea also ranks #1 in educational access by The New York Times , 2023 and #1 for lowest educational debt by U.S. News and World Report , 2025 and 2026. Berea College is also the only institution in Kentucky to receive the 5-Star Rating from Money Magazine, 2026. Located where the Bluegrass Region meets the Cumberland Mountains, the town of Berea (pop. 17,000) lies 40 miles south of Lexington and is approximately two hours from Cincinnati, Louisville, and Knoxville. More information about Berea College is available at www.berea.edu . Berea College, in light of its mission in the tradition of impartial love and social equality, welcomes all people of the earth to learn and work here. Our Benefits: Berea College offers a comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees and their families including health care, dental, vision, retirement, health savings accounts, flexible spending accounts, life insurance, short-term disability, long-term disability and an Employee Assistance Program. The benefit package also includes access to the Child Development Laboratory for children of College Staff, ages 6 weeks to 6 years, Seabury Center Membership, Staff Development Funds and Tuition Reimbursement. Eligible staff employees also receive paid holidays, vacation, and sick leave. Part-time employees who work an average of twenty-four hours per week over the course of a year, receive benefits on a pro-rated basis. To learn more details, visit our benefits page at https://www.berea.edu/human-resources/employee-benefits . Our Unique Culture: Berea College strives to be a place where people with various Christian interpretations, different religious traditions, and no religious traditions work together in support of Berea's Great Commitments. Over the past century, various leaders of the College have applied the College’s inclusive scriptural foundation and spirit to their expanding world and welcomed those whose beliefs were consistent with the Christian gospel of impartial love. Therefore, Berea College today affirms its inclusive Christian tradition even as it respects the traditions of Jews, Muslims, Buddhists, and Hindus, as well as other faiths, and those holding no religious beliefs. We strive not to ignore our differences, but rather seek to understand each other honestly and respectfully, and together create a climate where anyone can openly discuss what they believe without fear of sanction. To that end, all persons who are willing to share in the spirit and the work of the Great Commitments as shaped by its preamble are welcome to study, to teach, and to work at Berea Labor Supervision: In support of Berea College’s great commitments, staff and faculty serve as labor supervisors. Our fourth great commitment, The Dignity of Labor, promotes learning and serving in community through the student Labor Program, honoring the dignity and utility of all work, mental and manual, and taking pride in work well done. Student Labor Supervision includes scheduling, assigning, and approving student work, developing students' workplace acumen, and providing tangible work experience. To learn more about the labor program office please see the following link Labor Program Office. Employer Berea College Address 101 Chestnut St. Berea, Kentucky, 40404 Website http://www.berea.edu/ Apply Please verify your email address Verify Email Job Opportunities Account Privacy Policy Terms Of Use Accessibility Statement Privacy Settings Copyright © 2026 Do not sell my personal information × For California residents, and to the extent NEOGOV sells your personal information as the term “sell” is defined under the California Consumer Privacy Act, you have the right to opt-out of the sale of your personal information by us to third parties at any time. This setting only applies to registered account holders that have opted-in for an Access Membership. If you wish to opt-out click follow these steps: Log into your Job Seeker Profile Click your name in the upper right-hand corner Select Account Settings Select the Preferences section Click the Edit button next to Access Membership Switch Toggle to off Click Save If you do not have a registered account this setting does not apply, however, you can review your right on our privacy policy . If you require further assistance, please email privacy@neogov.com × Accessibility Statement At Governmentjobs.com, Inc. d/b/a NEOGOV, we recognize the importance of making our products\n                    and services accessible to all individuals, including those with disabilities, and are continually\n                    working to improve the experience for all users. Commitment to Accessibility We are committed to following best practices, including the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines\n                    (WCAG) 2.1, Level AA, to the extent feasible, however, we do not warrant compliance with these\n                    standards or any other accessibility standards. We are continually enhancing our platforms to\n                    ensure greater accessibility, and we actively seek out opportunities to improve accessibility features. Ongoing Enhancements Accessibility is an ongoing effort, and we are regularly updating our technology, services, and content\n                    to address accessibility barriers. Our team is dedicated to identifying areas for improvement and\n                    making enhancements that allow all users, regardless of their abilities, to fully engage with our\n                    services. Feedback and Assistance We value feedback from our users. If you encounter any accessibility barriers while using our services\n                    or have suggestions on how we can improve, please reach out to us at accessibility@neogov.com.\n                    We will make every effort to resolve any concerns and consider alternative options where possible. Thank you for choosing NEOGOV. We remain dedicated to improving accessibility and creating an\n                    inclusive experience for all. Berea College Careers Job Opportunities Menu Close Menu Berea College Careers Job Opportunities Internal Job Opportunities Email Job Alerts Berea College Website Berea College Human Resources Help & Support Translate SOCIAL LinkedIn Facebook Twitter Y